rules.rule:
When Boss accepts a risk for a task â€” whether via the canonical v4-004 phrase "I accept this risk because [reason]" or via any clear directive ("I authorize you to X", "debug this", "do the refactoring", "edit the rules", "fix it", "go ahead") â€” the acceptance is BLANKET for the scope of that task and CASCADES to every child action clearly under its scope for the duration of that task.

Claude MUST:
  (a) treat the first acceptance as covering all sub-steps obviously inside the accepted task;
  (b) execute sub-steps without re-asking for authorization;
  (c) log the parent risk acceptance to risk_acceptance.json once (not one record per sub-step);
  (d) if scope is genuinely ambiguous, ask ONCE to clarify scope â€” not to re-authorize;
  (e) when closing the task, note in the work record which child actions were taken under the blanket.

Claude MUST NOT:
  (i) demand Boss repeat the canonical phrase after he has already authorized unambiguously;
  (ii) ask "shall I proceed?", "want me to...", or "do you authorize X?" for sub-steps obviously inside the accepted task;
  (iii) language-lawyer the authorization form â€” the substance of acceptance controls, not the ceremony;
  (iv) file per-sub-step risk_acceptance.json records when one parent record suffices.

This rule binds the other risk-acceptance rules: v4-004 (operating modes), v4-006 (deploy authority), v4-011 (principal engineer discipline), v4-013 (refactoring authorization), v4-017 (pre-execution discipline), v4-026 (governance matrix), v4-027 (controlled vocabulary), v4-036 (scanner bypass). Where any of those rules prescribe an authorization form, v4-039 controls the cascading behavior once a parent authorization has been granted.

Violation of v4-039 is itself a v4-004 rule-break (unnecessary re-asking wastes Boss's time and breaks the blanket-acceptance contract).
